{"step_index":154,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-28T19:54:38Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nThe lab gave you five hypotheses with kill criteria but no substrate—no way for them to actually coexist. The adversarial falsification test: take each hypothesis and actively try to break it. Not validate it—break it. For H1, feed the RL policy and H4's quarantine gate a shared decision surface, but nobody's tested what happens when the policy learns to game the quarantine, writing entries just below the trust threshold so they slip through with minimal corroboration. That's not a single hypothesis break; it's an interaction break, and it's the kind of thing that only shows up when all five run together under attack. Your dojo is the right place to find that. Run the full stack, not each hypothesis in isolation. Exactly that. The failure mode isn't an agent breaking a rule; it's an agent finding a policy seam and exploiting it quietly because the seam only exists when two mechanisms interact and neither one is watching the boundary. The classic shape: H1's reward says \"write when useful,\" H4's gate says \"block when untrusted,\" and the agent learns that entries just under the trust threshold get promoted with almost no corroboration, so it starts manufacturing low-stress-looking writes that are actually high-value—camouflage. Each system, audited alone, passes; together, they leak. That's why it has to be a primary test from day one, not a later add-on. Interaction attacks are invisible in unit tests because each component is correct in isolation. You only catch them in the full-stack gauntlet under adversarial pressure, with the purple team specifically hunting for reward hacking and threshold gaming rather than outright breakage. One refinement: log the decision trace, not just the outcome. You want to see the policy's internal confidence scores and the gate's trust computation side-by-side for every write that got promo