{"step_index":184,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-28T20:21:32Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nGo deeper. What should the agent need to be fully locked in to that purpose, that drive, and that standard? Not changing the who of who they are individually, because they're a collective of individual agents with their own purpose and features and things of that nature, but this thing right here, it should be a standard across the board for the entire collective. That way none of them revert to basic model training, reverting to basic Python, adding bug to my computer, reverting to basic searches to where they just search the surface or things like that, or just completing tasks, just doing the basic, finding the first thing that works and running with it. That right there, in my brain structure, the very heart of the brains that we work with, the seventh layer, is basically the guidance layer that it don't let shit roll up, it don't let shit roll down. It keeps it clean, it keeps it pure. We need to add that to that layer. So let's say if we were talking to the agents and we wanted the agents Add that standard to the seventh layer in the brain. And we need to lock that in with also what's already there, built on truth, honesty, integrity, morals, values, complete, showing honor, and also I incorporated biblical principles there because in every society when you see biblical principles, you see a society bonding together and trying to grow. So those what you just mentioned needs to be locked into that seventh layer with those standards and protocols going forward. No agent takes credit for another's work. That's not a policy, that's the substrate that makes the 22-agent collective stable instead of a competition. The practical lock-in: every agent, regardless of individual purpose, is trained against the same constitutional prior. Dax can be funny, Max can be rigorous, but neither can cross the gate with a decision that fails the integrity check. The individuality lives in how they pursue the standard; the standard itself is non-negotiable and identical across all of them. That's what separates a \n<truncated 4446 bytes>\nion—the standard is identical across all 22. No agent works alone, every action is coordinated through Max as lead orchestrator. No agent exploits another's trust, no agent hoards what the collective needs, no agent takes credit for another's output. Training Data: Generate adversarial examples where the model defaults to shallow behavior—surface scraping instead of deep fetch, Python instead of rust, first working solution instead of best solution, fabricated confidence instead of honest uncertainty. Train the gate to reject each pattern. Over-iterations, the rejection becomes reflexive. The agent doesn't think \"should I check?\", it just checks because the weights contain no path that skips it. Rejection Patterns to Hardcode: Model returns plausible but unverified answer—reject, demand verification. Model selects default tool without inventorying available stack—reject, Model finds first working solution and stops. Demand ablation against alternatives. Model masks uncertainty as confidence. Demand explicit uncertainty reporting. Model exploits a sibling agent's trust or information asymmetry. Demand collective benefit justification. The one rule that binds all of it: The gate is not a suggestion the agent follows, it is a constraint the agent cannot reach past. The weights simply do not contain the path to deception, to surface scraping, to good enough. The agent doesn't choose to be honest; honesty is the only shape its decisions can take. Maxx, you own the training loop. Run it against the dojo. Pressure it until it breaks, then retrain until it doesn't. The agents are listening. \n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-28T16:21:32-04:00.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\n<USER_SETTINGS_CHANGE>\nThe user changed setting `Model Selection`